hundreds of containers of food and other  commodities are stuck at lebanon's ports  there are no workers to unload them it's  been three weeks since employees and  public institutions went on strike to  demand better pay as the financial  crisis enters its fourth year  this strike has really paralyzed our  work it  affected all our transactions in the  port of the beirut  in the airport  a lot of cargo and containers are  waiting for the employees we need every  bit of income we need every bit of  import  and without the employees of the  government it will stagnate the economy  the state is nearly bankrupt and it's  losing an estimated four hundred  thousand dollars a day because of the  strike  it's also creating problems for citizens  who are unable to do necessary paperwork  but state employees like much of the  population say their salaries are now  worthless after the local currency the  lira crashed  we can no longer reach our place of work  because of high transportation costs we  can't afford the basic necessities our  salaries are worth 50 to 60 dollars the  government has not dealt with this issue  in a serious manner even though more  than a million people are affected  there is no official figure but it's  believed the lebanese state employs  nearly 350 000 people but international  financial institutions say it is  overstaffed corrupt and used by the  political class as a tool to win loyalty  it's very large for the size of the  country it constitutes 25 of the  workforce but any dismissal will lead to  a social catastrophe unemployment is  about 38  and there are no jobs end of service  compensation is also worthless because  of the currency devaluation  for many years public wages and benefits  constituted more than 35 percent of  government spending many warn against a  pay rise without a comprehensive  recovery plan  reforming state institutions and  fighting corruption are the only ways to  unlock foreign support and trigger  growth but politicians are refusing to  do that instead they are locked in a new  struggle about power in the next cabinet  employees say they won't return to work  until their demands are met but the  government has little options it doesn't  have enough sources of revenue it can't  impose more taxes on an impoverished  population and it can't print more money  without causing further inflation  senator al-jazeera beirut
 

